WEBVTT

00:00.227 --> 00:02.509
- [Voiceover] What I'd like to
introduce you to in this video

00:02.509 --> 00:07.483
is the idea of a Linear Equation.

00:08.493 --> 00:11.206
And just to start ourselves out,

00:11.206 --> 00:14.260
let's look at some examples
of linear equations.

00:14.260 --> 00:16.280
So, for example the equation

00:16.280 --> 00:19.704
y is equal to two x minus three,

00:19.704 --> 00:22.607
this is a linear equation.

00:22.607 --> 00:25.439
Now why do we call it a linear equation?

00:25.439 --> 00:27.850
Well if you were to take
the set of all of the

00:27.850 --> 00:30.903
xy pairs that satisfy this equation

00:30.903 --> 00:33.387
and if you were to graph
them on the coordinate plane,

00:33.387 --> 00:35.709
you would actually get a line.

00:35.709 --> 00:37.764
That's why it's called a linear equation.

00:37.764 --> 00:39.946
And let's actually feel
good about that statement.

00:39.946 --> 00:43.589
Let's see, let's plot some of the xy pairs

00:43.589 --> 00:46.283
that satisfy this equation
and then feel good

00:46.283 --> 00:48.639
that it does indeed generate a line.

00:48.639 --> 00:50.436
So, I'm just gonna pick some x values and

00:50.436 --> 00:53.617
make it easy to calculate
the corresponding y values.

00:53.617 --> 00:55.672
So, if x is equal to zero

00:55.672 --> 00:58.771
y is gonna be two times zero minus three

00:58.771 --> 01:00.652
which is negative three.

01:00.652 --> 01:03.090
And on our coordinate plane here that's--

01:03.090 --> 01:04.773
we're gonna move zero in the x direction,

01:04.773 --> 01:06.154
zero in the horizontal direction

01:06.154 --> 01:08.168
and we're gonna go down three
in the vertical direction,

01:08.168 --> 01:09.178
in the y direction.

01:09.178 --> 01:11.406
So, that's that point there.

01:11.406 --> 01:14.611
If x is equal to one, what is y equal to?

01:14.611 --> 01:19.611
Well two times one is two,
minus three is negative one.

01:19.707 --> 01:22.110
So we move positive one in the x direction

01:22.110 --> 01:25.790
and negative one, or down
one, in the y direction.

01:25.790 --> 01:29.772
Now let's see, if x is
equal to two what is y?

01:29.772 --> 01:33.231
Two times two is four, minus three is one.

01:33.231 --> 01:37.654
When x is equal to two y is equal to one.

01:37.654 --> 01:38.955
And hopefully you're seeing now

01:38.955 --> 01:40.275
that if I were to keep going,

01:40.275 --> 01:41.690
and I encourage you though if you want

01:41.690 --> 01:43.541
pause the video and try x equals three

01:43.541 --> 01:45.284
or x equals negative one and keep going.

01:45.284 --> 01:48.737
You will see that this is
going to generate a line.

01:48.737 --> 01:50.305
And in fact, let me connect these dots

01:50.305 --> 01:53.903
and you will see the line
that I'm talking about.

01:53.903 --> 01:57.177
So, let me see if I can draw,

01:57.177 --> 01:59.023
I'm gonna use the line tool here.

01:59.023 --> 02:02.483
Try to connect the dots
as neatly as I can.

02:02.483 --> 02:03.342
There you go.

02:03.342 --> 02:06.035
This line that I have just drawn,

02:06.035 --> 02:08.438
this is the graph,

02:08.438 --> 02:12.803
this is the graph of y is
equal to two x minus three.

02:12.803 --> 02:15.519
So if you were to graph all
of the xy pairs that satisfy

02:15.519 --> 02:18.782
this equation you are gonna get this line.

02:18.782 --> 02:20.360
And you might be saying,
"Hey wait wait, hold on Sal,

02:20.360 --> 02:22.079
you just tried some particular points,

02:22.079 --> 02:23.474
why don't I just get a bunch of points,

02:23.474 --> 02:24.830
how do I actually get a line?"

02:24.830 --> 02:26.165
Well, I just tried,

02:26.165 --> 02:29.214
over here I just tried
integer values of x.

02:29.214 --> 02:31.609
But you can try any value in
between here, all of these,

02:31.609 --> 02:33.304
it's actually a pretty unique concept.

02:33.304 --> 02:36.067
Any value of x that you input into this,

02:36.067 --> 02:38.273
you find the corresponding value for y,

02:38.273 --> 02:40.432
it will sit on this line.

02:40.432 --> 02:41.906
So for example,

02:41.906 --> 02:43.694
for example,

02:43.694 --> 02:47.270
if we were to take x is equal to,

02:47.270 --> 02:51.588
actually let's say x is
equal to negative point five.

02:51.588 --> 02:53.887
So if x is equal to negative point five

02:53.887 --> 02:55.291
if we look at the line

02:55.291 --> 02:57.903
when x is equal to negative point five

02:57.903 --> 02:59.424
it looks like

02:59.424 --> 03:04.424
it looks like y is equal to negative four.

03:04.671 --> 03:06.575
And that looks like that sits on the line.

03:06.575 --> 03:07.647
Well let's verify that.

03:07.647 --> 03:09.305
If x is equal to negative,

03:09.305 --> 03:11.859
I'll write that as negative one half,

03:11.859 --> 03:13.577
then what is y equal to?

03:13.577 --> 03:16.515
Let's see, two times negative one half,

03:16.515 --> 03:17.102
I'll just write it out,

03:17.102 --> 03:18.344
two times negative--

03:18.344 --> 03:23.344
two times negative one half minus three.

03:23.603 --> 03:25.379
Well this says two times
negative one half is

03:25.379 --> 03:29.141
negative one minus three
is indeed negative four

03:29.141 --> 03:31.660
it is indeed negative four.

03:31.660 --> 03:34.060
So you can literally take any, any--

03:34.060 --> 03:35.869
for any x value that you put here

03:35.869 --> 03:37.309
and the corresponding y value

03:37.309 --> 03:39.062
it is going to sit on the line.

03:39.062 --> 03:41.883
This point right over
here represents a solution

03:41.883 --> 03:43.707
to this linear equation.

03:43.707 --> 03:45.506
Let me do this in a color you can see.

03:45.506 --> 03:47.489
So this point represents a
solution to a linear equation.

03:47.489 --> 03:50.245
This point represents a
solution to a linear equation.

03:50.245 --> 03:53.786
This point is not a solution
to a linear equation.

03:53.786 --> 03:55.621
So if ex is equal to five

03:55.621 --> 03:58.441
then y is not gonna be equal to three.

03:58.441 --> 04:00.623
If x is gonna be equal to
five you go to the line to see

04:00.623 --> 04:02.674
what the solution to
the linear equation is.

04:02.674 --> 04:07.097
If x is five this shows us
that y is going to be seven.

04:07.097 --> 04:08.261
And it's indeed--

04:08.261 --> 04:09.654
that's indeed the case.

04:09.654 --> 04:12.638
Two times five is ten,
minus three is seven.

04:12.638 --> 04:13.393
The point--

04:13.393 --> 04:17.618
the point five comma seven is on,

04:17.618 --> 04:19.292
or it satisfies this linear equation.

04:19.292 --> 04:21.715
So if you take all of the
xy pairs that satisfy it,

04:21.715 --> 04:22.910
you get a line.

04:22.910 --> 04:25.289
That is why it is called
a linear equation.

04:25.289 --> 04:27.507
Now, this isn't the only
way that we could write

04:27.507 --> 04:28.057
a linear equation.

04:28.057 --> 04:30.657
You could write a linear equation like--

04:30.657 --> 04:32.375
let me do this in a new color.

04:32.375 --> 04:34.291
You could write a linear
equation like this:

04:34.291 --> 04:38.618
Four x minus three y is equal to twelve.

04:38.618 --> 04:42.315
This also is a linear equation.

04:42.315 --> 04:46.513
And we can see that if we
were to graph the xy pairs

04:46.513 --> 04:49.090
that satisfy this we would
once again get a line.

04:49.090 --> 04:50.495
X and y.

04:50.495 --> 04:53.176
If x is equal to zero,

04:53.176 --> 04:54.270
then this goes away and

04:54.270 --> 04:57.253
you have negative three
y is equal to twelve.

04:57.253 --> 04:59.650
Let's see, if negative
three y equals twelve

04:59.650 --> 05:03.306
then y would be equal to negative four.

05:03.306 --> 05:05.907
Nega-- zero comma negative four.

05:05.907 --> 05:06.766
You can verify that.

05:06.766 --> 05:09.832
Four times zero minus
three times negative four

05:09.832 --> 05:12.547
well that's gonna be
equal to positive twelve.

05:12.547 --> 05:15.508
And let's see, if y were to equal zero,

05:15.508 --> 05:17.005
if y were to equal zero

05:17.005 --> 05:19.884
then this is gonna be four
times x is equal to twelve,

05:19.884 --> 05:21.509
well then x is equal to three.

05:21.509 --> 05:25.201
And so you have the point
zero comma negative four,

05:25.201 --> 05:29.445
zero comma negative four on this line,

05:29.445 --> 05:33.776
and you have the point three
comma zero on this line.

05:33.776 --> 05:36.318
Three comma zero.

05:36.318 --> 05:37.432
Did I do that right?

05:37.432 --> 05:37.682
Yep.

05:37.682 --> 05:41.506
So zero comma negative four
and then three comma zero.

05:41.506 --> 05:44.304
These are going to be on this line.

05:44.304 --> 05:47.624
Three comma zero is also on this line.

05:47.624 --> 05:50.863
So this is, this line is
going to look something like--

05:50.863 --> 05:53.452
something like, I'll
just try to hand draw it.

05:53.452 --> 05:56.412
Something like that.

05:56.412 --> 05:59.024
So once again, all of the xy--

05:59.024 --> 06:02.089
all of the xy pairs that satisfy this,

06:02.089 --> 06:04.934
if you were to plot them
out it forms a line.

06:04.934 --> 06:07.259
Now what are some examples,
maybe you're saying

06:07.259 --> 06:09.976
"Wait, wait, wait, isn't any
equation a linear equation?"

06:09.976 --> 06:12.681
And the simple answer is
"No, not any equation is

06:12.681 --> 06:13.354
a linear equation."

06:13.354 --> 06:15.943
I'll give you some examples
of non-linear equations.

06:15.943 --> 06:18.044
So a non--

06:18.044 --> 06:20.122
non-linear,

06:20.122 --> 06:22.920
whoops let me write a
little bit neater than that.

06:22.920 --> 06:27.575
Non-linear equations.

06:27.575 --> 06:29.054
Well, those could include something like

06:29.054 --> 06:31.109
y is equal to x-squared.

06:31.109 --> 06:31.821
If you graph this

06:31.821 --> 06:34.642
you will see that this
is going to be a curve.

06:34.642 --> 06:39.146
it could be something like x
times y is equal to twelve.

06:39.146 --> 06:41.236
This is also not going to be a line.

06:41.236 --> 06:46.236
Or it could be something
like five over x plus y

06:46.785 --> 06:48.724
is equal to ten.

06:48.724 --> 06:52.415
This also is not going to be a line.

06:52.415 --> 06:54.222
So now, and at some point you could--

06:54.222 --> 06:55.778
I encourage you to try
to graph these things,

06:55.778 --> 06:57.415
they're actually quite interesting.

06:57.415 --> 07:00.735
But given that we've now seen
examples of linear equations

07:00.735 --> 07:03.057
and non-linear equations,

07:03.057 --> 07:04.933
let's see if we can come
up with a definition for

07:04.933 --> 07:05.757
linear equations.

07:05.757 --> 07:07.649
One way to think about is it's an equation

07:07.649 --> 07:09.751
that if you were to graph
all of the x and y pairs that

07:09.751 --> 07:11.028
satisfy this equation,

07:11.028 --> 07:12.316
you'll get a line.

07:12.316 --> 07:13.415
And that's actually
literally where the word

07:13.415 --> 07:15.191
linear equation comes from.

07:15.191 --> 07:16.805
But another way to think about it is

07:16.805 --> 07:18.407
it's going to be an equation where

07:18.407 --> 07:21.146
every term is either
going to be a constant,

07:21.146 --> 07:23.054
so for example, twelve is a constant.

07:23.054 --> 07:24.967
It's not going to change
based on the value of

07:24.967 --> 07:27.232
some variable, twelve is twelve.

07:27.232 --> 07:29.495
Or negative three is negative three.

07:29.495 --> 07:31.550
So every term is either
going to be a constant

07:31.550 --> 07:35.416
or it's going to be a
constant times a variable

07:35.416 --> 07:37.111
raised to the first power.

07:37.111 --> 07:40.710
So this is the constant two
times x to the first power.

07:40.710 --> 07:43.670
This is the variable y
raised to the first power.

07:43.670 --> 07:46.131
You could say that bceause
this is just one y.

07:46.131 --> 07:48.476
We're not dividing by x or y,

07:48.476 --> 07:49.950
we're not multiplying,

07:49.950 --> 07:52.853
we don't have a term that
has x to the second power,

07:52.853 --> 07:55.374
or x to the third power,
or y to the fifth power.

07:55.374 --> 07:57.568
We just have y to the first power,

07:57.568 --> 07:58.671
we have x to the first power.

07:58.671 --> 08:00.579
We're not multiplying x and y together

08:00.579 --> 08:02.227
like we did over here.

08:02.227 --> 08:03.226
So if every--

08:03.226 --> 08:05.164
if every term in your equation,

08:05.164 --> 08:06.363
on either side of the equation,

08:06.363 --> 08:10.566
is either a constant or its
just some number times x,

08:10.566 --> 08:13.406
just x to the first power
or some number times y,

08:13.406 --> 08:15.554
and you're not multiplying
your x's and y's together

08:15.554 --> 08:18.903
you are dealing with a linear equation.
